  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 310 PM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Seasonable to slightly above average temperatures \nand dry conditions through Saturday.\n\nThe latest wx analysis continues to show a split flow pattern with a \nnorthern stream trough across the Northeast and a southern stream \nevident in the cloud pattern to the N and NE of Hurricane Isaias, \nwith a trough in the vicinity of TX. Sunny skies are evident over \nmost of the area, with scattered CU over the piedmont and high \ncirrus clouds moving into NE NC from the southern stream. A gradual \nincrease in high level clouds will begin tonight, as upper level \nmoisture spreads over the region well in advance of Isaias. \nTemperatures are warm today with observations in the lower 80s, \nwhich could still rise another degree or so today before reaching \nthe high. Today looks to be the warmest day of the week with temps \ntrending down towards seasonal averages with highs on Friday in the \nupper 70s and in the lower to mid 70s on Saturday. Temps will likely \nbe a few degrees cooler along the coast both days. Not necessarily \nimpactful, but statistical guidance is on the order of 4-8F cooler \nthan NBM guidance for lows late Friday night/early Saturday morning \n(lower 50s to lower 60s NBM vs. mid/upper 40s to upper 50s MOS). Dry \nconditions continue through Saturday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Moisture and rain chances return Saturday night\ninto early Monday.\n\nBy Sunday and Monday, there is increasing confidence that moisture \ngets pulled northward and then pushed eastward across the region as \nIsaias makes landfall and merges with an upper trough in vicinity of \nthe Mid-South. Confidence in timing has increased some as the model \nguidance has came into better agreement for the onset of accumulated \nrainfall, showing the 06-12z Sunday timeframe. The 12z ensembles \ncontinue to show PW values aob 200% of normal along with a plume of \ninstability surging N ahead of the trough. This could lead to heavy \nrainfall for areas and the chance for thunderstorms on Sunday. There \nis still spread between the 10th and 90th percentile QPF in the \n12z/08 ensemble guidance, but amounts generally trended up. In \ngeneral, the 10th percentile (reasonable low-end) accumulated precip \nthrough 00z Tuesday is 0.25-0.75\", while the 90th percentile is on \nthe order of 2.0-4.0\". The 50th percentile (most likely case) shows \na generalized 1.0-2.0\" of rainfall, with the highest rainfall totals \nin the southern half of the area as there is a hint in the guidance \nof more enhanced rainfall along a coastal trough. The presence of a \ncoastal trough would also suggest a band of drier air setting up \nsomewhere over the area. This could lead to a large spread in \nrainfall totals across the area. The ECMWF, ECMWF ens, GFS, AI GFS, \nand the CMC all hint at portions of inland areas only receiving ~1.0-\n1.5\" with totals near the coast amounting to ~2.0-4.0\". The coastal \ntrough would also suggest an in situ wedge resulting in cooler \ntemperatures and more stable conditions Sunday across the Piedmont. \nDeeper moisture erodes Sunday night into Monday. However, there are \nlingering PoPs as the upper trough swings across the region. Behind \nthis system, drier air will filter back in later Monday through the \nmiddle of the week. Temperatures should remain near average late \nthis weekend through the middle of next week.",
